-- tcl/tests/ares/rsp_task_vacc.pk64 -- the accumulator, written in Pak.
--
-- Step 3 of docs/rsp-microcode-in-pak.md's suggested order: "vacc. The
-- multiply family and the checker rules that keep its call order honest."
-- This is the design note's own worked shape (`vacc.mul` then `vacc.mac`
-- then a reader), minus the loop -- just two terms, enough to prove VMULF
-- (fresh, rounded) and VMACF (accumulate) chain correctly into one
-- accumulator that `vacc.mid()` reads back with VSAR.
--
-- m0 = m1 = v = 0.5 in every lane (Q1.15, 0x4000): mul(m0, v.broadcast(0))
-- contributes 0.25, mac(m1, v.broadcast(1)) contributes another 0.25, so
-- every lane of result should read back 0.5 (0x4000) via vacc.mid().
--
-- Compiled by tcl/rsp_codegen.tcl (pak::rsp_generate_records). DMEM layout
-- is the bump allocator, in declaration order: m0=0, m1=16, v=32,
-- result=48. The driver must agree, and does -- see
-- rsp_task_vacc_driver.pk64.

use rsp.vacc

static m0: vec8x16
static m1: vec8x16
static v: vec8x16
static result: vec8x16

entry {
    vacc.mul(m0, v.broadcast(0))
    vacc.mac(m1, v.broadcast(1))
    result = vacc.mid()
}
